From 90722a536393b6135696c6d8f87d1586295017c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan Tsai <alan-tsai@quantatw.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 17:07:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] spi: Fix output pipe creation for asyncSystem callback

The asyncSystem() function supports capturing command output through
either a result string or an output callback. However, the output pipe
is only created when a result string is provided.

When only an output callback is supplied, captureOutput is true but
the output pipe remains uninitialized. This causes the parent process
to use an invalid file descriptor and prevents the callback from
receiving command output.

Fix the pipe creation condition to use captureOutput, ensuring that
the output pipe is created when either a result string or an output
callback is provided.

This fixes the BIOS flashcp progress monitoring introduced by
commit b406a15, where asyncSystem() is called with only an output
callback to update ActivationProgress.

Fixes: "Change-Id : Id774da76cbd8174be271c14202c280f2e30c0516"
("spi: Report flashcp update progress during BIOS update")

Tested on rainiera7:
'''
1.load factory default On rainiera7 & power off the host.

2.flashed 134.2MB bios image by redfish command.

Step 1: Set Environment Variables
export bmc=10.10.12.56 # Target BMC IP
export creds="-k -u user:password" # BMC login credentials
export fwpath="firmware_name.pldm" # Firmware image file path
export target="Rainier_BIOS" # Redfish component target (prefix match supported)
export applytime="Immediate" # Apply time: Immediate or OnReset

Step 2: Identify Firmware Inventory Path
export targetpath=$(curl --silent $creds https://$bmc/redfish/v1/UpdateService/FirmwareInventory/ | \
jq -r '.Members[]."@odata.id"' | \
grep "^/redfish/v1/UpdateService/FirmwareInventory/${target}")

Step 3: Upload and Apply the Firmware
curl -k $creds \
  -H "Content-Type:multipart/form-data" \
  -X POST \
  -F UpdateParameters="{\"Targets\":[\"$targetpath\"],\"@Redfish.OperationApplyTime\":\"$applytime\"};type=application/json" \
  -F "UpdateFile=@$fwpath;type=application/octet-stream" \
  https://${bmc}/redfish/v1/UpdateService/update-multipart

Step 4: Check Firmware Update Progress
curl -k $creds -H 'Content-Type: application/json' https://$bmc/redfish/v1/TaskService/Tasks/0

